# CONV toolchain - what the assembler, the linker and the dynamic loader share
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Tier CONVENTION. The toolchain is judged through the command-line tools a
# system provides (found with command -v: c17 or cc, as, ld, ar), by
# building tiny objects and reading the result with a reader of the common
# object-file container (conv/lib/elfdump.c, written from the gABI field
# layout). A test is NOTINUSE when a tool is absent or the compiler refuses
# an option beyond the POSIX compiler utility (-shared -fPIC -Wl,...; probed
# once per run); it never names an operating system.
#
# RULE for this area: assert only what at least two independent toolchain
# documents state the same way. The documents (source key; conv/STATUS.md
# gives the hosts):
#   GAS   the GNU assembler manual (directives, i386 syntax)
#   GLD   the GNU linker manual (options)
#   LLX   the LLVM "Extensions" document (assembler syntax); LLNM the LLVM
#         nm command guide (symbol classes)
#   ULD   ld(1) of a second, independent linker family; URT its runtime
#         linker manual ld.so.1(1); UDL its dlopen(3C) and dlclose(3C)
#   GABI  the System V generic ABI (chapters 4 and 5: sections, symbols,
#         dynamic linking); PSABI the x86-64 supplement (sources)
# Assembler directives are described in full by GAS only (LLX documents the
# extensions of its integrated assembler and uses the same syntax in its
# examples). Each directive assertion therefore rests on GAS (what the
# directive does) together with the GABI definition of what the object must
# then contain, and LLX where it shows the same spelling; the data directives
# themselves (bytes emitted, string terminators, alignment padding) have no
# second description and are listed under "not assertable" in conv/README.md.
# A failure is a deviation for a person to judge (INSPECT), not a
# conformance bug.
#
# Deliberately not asserted (README has the reasons): AT&T operand order and
# prefixes; .byte .short .long .quad .ascii .asciz .align and the bytes they
# emit; numeric local labels; the conventional symbols etext edata end; order
# of init-array entries inside one object and finalisation order; LD_PRELOAD;
# whether LD_LIBRARY_PATH is searched before or after a recorded run path;
# constructors run by dlopen and destructors run by dlclose; the symbol
# lookup order among a program's dependencies; the linker of the LLVM project
# (its own documentation could not be reached).

assertion  CONV.toolchain.001
anchor     as.html#.text
anchor     as.html#.data
anchor     as.html#.bss
anchor     gabi/ch4.sheader.html#special-sections
class      disputed
says       After .text, .data and .bss an assembled object has sections named
           .text, .data and .bss, of type progbits, progbits and nobits, with the
           attributes allocated and executable (text), allocated and writable
           (data) and allocated and writable (bss); a label defined after each
           directive lies in that section.
test       t/001-asm-sections.sh
note       tier CONVENTION. GAS: each directive assembles the following
           statements onto the end of the section of that name. GABI special
           sections: .text holds the instructions (progbits, alloc + exec), .data
           initialised data (progbits, alloc + write), .bss uninitialised data
           (nobits, alloc + write). The two agree on names and roles; the
           attribute letters are the GABI's. The statements put into the sections
           (.byte, .skip) only make the sections non-empty: the bytes are not
           asserted. Sources agree.
nomutant   runs the system's own assembler, linker and loader; faults are planted
           by substituting a wrong tool (conv/neg/run.sh, PC_CONV_AS, PC_CONV_LD,
           PC_CONV_CC)

assertion  CONV.toolchain.002
anchor     as.html#.section
anchor     llvm-extensions.html#.section
anchor     gabi/ch4.sheader.html#sh_type
class      disputed
says       A .section directive with the flags "a", "aw" or "ax" and the type
           progbits, or "aw" and the type nobits, creates a section with the
           attributes allocated, allocated + writable, allocated + executable,
           and with the type named; no other attribute is added.
test       t/002-asm-section-flags.sh
note       tier CONVENTION. GAS lists the ELF flag letters a (allocatable), w
           (writable), x (executable) and the @type argument; LLX shows .section
           with "a", "ax" and @progbits in its ELF examples and says its flags
           are compatible with GAS; GABI defines the attributes and the progbits
           and nobits types. The character that introduces the type word is a
           comment character on some processors (GAS says so), so the at sign,
           the percent sign and the hash sign are tried in turn; the test fails
           only when none is accepted. Sources agree.
nomutant   runs the system's own assembler, linker and loader; faults are planted
           by substituting a wrong tool (conv/neg/run.sh, PC_CONV_AS, PC_CONV_LD,
           PC_CONV_CC)

assertion  CONV.toolchain.003
anchor     as.html#.global
anchor     as.html#.weak
anchor     as.html#.local
anchor     gabi/ch4.symtab.html#symbol-binding
anchor     llvm-nm.html#weak
class      disputed
says       In an assembled object a symbol named by .globl is global, one named
           by .weak is weak, one named by .local or by no directive is local; all
           are defined; every local symbol precedes the others in the symbol
           table and the table's info field is the index of the first non-local
           symbol.
test       t/003-asm-binding.sh
note       tier CONVENTION. GAS: .globl makes the symbol visible to ld, .weak
           sets the weak attribute, .local makes a symbol local so that it is not
           externally visible. GABI: local, global (visible to all combined
           objects) and weak bindings; all locals precede the weak and global
           symbols and the symbol table's info field names the first non-local
           one. LLNM: weak symbols have lower precedence than regular ones.
           Sources agree.
nomutant   runs the system's own assembler, linker and loader; faults are planted
           by substituting a wrong tool (conv/neg/run.sh, PC_CONV_AS, PC_CONV_LD,
           PC_CONV_CC)

assertion  CONV.toolchain.004
anchor     as.html#.type
anchor     as.html#.size
anchor     gabi/ch4.symtab.html#symbol-types
class      disputed
says       After .type with a function description a symbol has the function
           type, after .type with an object description the object type; .size (a
           number or label arithmetic) sets the size the symbol records; a symbol
           with neither directive has no type and size zero.
test       t/004-asm-type-size.sh
note       tier CONVENTION. GAS: .type sets a symbol to a function or an object
           symbol (five spellings, some of which are comment characters on some
           processors, so the spellings are tried in turn and the test fails only
           when none is accepted), .size sets the size from an expression that
           may use label arithmetic. GABI: the function and object symbol types;
           a symbol's size is its byte count and zero when it has none or an
           unknown one. Sources agree.
nomutant   runs the system's own assembler, linker and loader; faults are planted
           by substituting a wrong tool (conv/neg/run.sh, PC_CONV_AS, PC_CONV_LD,
           PC_CONV_CC)

assertion  CONV.toolchain.005
anchor     as.html#.comm
anchor     gabi/ch4.symtab.html#special-section-indexes
anchor     llvm-nm.html#C
class      disputed
says       After .comm NAME,SIZE,ALIGN an assembled object holds a global symbol
           NAME defined relative to the common section index, with size SIZE and
           with the alignment as its value; the alignment is optional.
test       t/005-asm-comm.sh
note       tier CONVENTION. GAS: .comm declares a common symbol, takes an
           optional third argument that is the alignment in bytes on ELF; GABI: a
           symbol in the common section index labels a block not yet allocated,
           its value gives the alignment and its size the bytes required (the
           symbol type may be object or common). LLNM: a common symbol. Sources
           agree.
nomutant   runs the system's own assembler, linker and loader; faults are planted
           by substituting a wrong tool (conv/neg/run.sh, PC_CONV_AS, PC_CONV_LD,
           PC_CONV_CC)

assertion  CONV.toolchain.006
anchor     ld.html#-o
anchor     uld-ld.1#-o
class      disputed
says       ld -o NAME writes the output as the file NAME and creates no other
           output file; without -o the output is a file named a.out.
test       t/006-ld-output-name.sh
note       tier CONVENTION. GLD: -o names the output, a.out by default. ULD: -o
           outfile, default a.out. Sources agree. The output of a static link is
           an executable object file (type exec) or, on a toolchain that defaults
           to position-independent output, a shared-object type; both are
           accepted.
nomutant   runs the system's own assembler, linker and loader; faults are planted
           by substituting a wrong tool (conv/neg/run.sh, PC_CONV_AS, PC_CONV_LD,
           PC_CONV_CC)

assertion  CONV.toolchain.007
anchor     ld.html#-l
anchor     ld.html#-L
anchor     uld-ld.1#-l
anchor     uld-ld.1#-L
class      disputed
says       With ld -L DIR... -l NAME, a directory holding both a shared object
           libNAME.so and an archive libNAME.a gives the shared object; when only
           the first directory has the archive and a later one has the shared
           object, the first directory wins (the archive is used); when the
           shared object is in the first directory it is used.
test       t/007-ld-library-search.sh
note       tier CONVENTION. GLD: -l searches the library path for libNAME.so
           before libNAME.a in a directory; -L directories are searched in
           command-line order. ULD: ld chooses libNAME.so if both forms exist;
           the directory search stops at the first directory containing either.
           The sources differ on whether -L must precede the -l it applies to
           (GLD: all -L apply to all -l; ULD: only those that precede), so the
           test always places -L first. Sources agree on what is asserted.
nomutant   runs the system's own assembler, linker and loader; faults are planted
           by substituting a wrong tool (conv/neg/run.sh, PC_CONV_AS, PC_CONV_LD,
           PC_CONV_CC)

assertion  CONV.toolchain.008
anchor     ld.html#-l
anchor     uld-ld.1#archive-processing
anchor     gabi/ch4.symtab.html#symbol-binding
class      disputed
says       A member of an archive is taken into the link only if it defines a
           symbol that is undefined where the archive appears on the command
           line: a member defining only unneeded symbols is left out, an archive
           placed before the object that needs it does not satisfy it, and a weak
           undefined reference does not pull a member out.
test       t/008-ld-archive.sh
note       tier CONVENTION. GLD: an archive is searched once, at the place where
           it is given; a member is included when it defines a symbol undefined
           in an earlier object, and an undefined symbol in a later object does
           not cause a second search. ULD: a library is searched exactly once at
           the point it is met; only routines that define an unresolved reference
           are loaded; weak references do not trigger extraction. GABI: the link
           editor extracts members that define undefined global symbols and does
           not extract them to resolve undefined weak symbols. Three documents
           agree. The linker of the LLVM project is documented elsewhere (not
           reachable) and was observed to accept the archive-first order: see
           conv/known-deviations.md.
nomutant   runs the system's own assembler, linker and loader; faults are planted
           by substituting a wrong tool (conv/neg/run.sh, PC_CONV_AS, PC_CONV_LD,
           PC_CONV_CC)

assertion  CONV.toolchain.009
anchor     gabi/ch4.symtab.html#symbol-binding
anchor     llvm-nm.html#W
class      disputed
says       When a symbol has a strong definition in one object and a weak
           definition in another, the output has the strong one and the symbol is
           not weak, in either order of the objects; a weak reference that
           nothing defines does not make the link fail, and if the symbol is kept
           it has value zero.
test       t/009-ld-weak-strong.sh
note       tier CONVENTION. GABI: if a defined global symbol exists, a weak
           symbol of the same name is not an error and the global definition is
           honoured; unresolved weak symbols have value zero. LLNM: a weak
           definition is used only if no regular definition exists in the link;
           an undefined weak symbol is not a link failure. GLD and ULD say little
           about weak definitions beyond archives. Two documents agree.
nomutant   runs the system's own assembler, linker and loader; faults are planted
           by substituting a wrong tool (conv/neg/run.sh, PC_CONV_AS, PC_CONV_LD,
           PC_CONV_CC)

assertion  CONV.toolchain.010
anchor     gabi/ch4.symtab.html#symbol-binding
anchor     ld.html#-z-muldefs
anchor     uld-ld.1#-z-muldefs
class      disputed
says       Two strong definitions of one global symbol in different objects make
           the link fail, in either order of the objects.
test       t/010-ld-duplicate.sh
note       tier CONVENTION. GABI: the link editor does not allow multiple
           definitions of global symbols. GLD: normally the linker reports a
           fatal error when a symbol is defined several times (-z muldefs relaxes
           that). ULD: by default multiple definitions between relocatable
           objects are a fatal error. Three documents agree. The test checks the
           exit status only.
nomutant   runs the system's own assembler, linker and loader; faults are planted
           by substituting a wrong tool (conv/neg/run.sh, PC_CONV_AS, PC_CONV_LD,
           PC_CONV_CC)

assertion  CONV.toolchain.011
anchor     ld.html#-unresolved-symbols
anchor     uld-ld.1#-z-defs
anchor     uld-ld.1#-r
class      disputed
says       ld fails when an executable would contain an undefined reference; the
           same object is accepted when the output is a shared object (-shared)
           or a relocatable object (-r).
test       t/011-ld-undefined.sh
note       tier CONVENTION. GLD: unresolved symbols are reported as errors by
           default (report-all); the shared-library case is the non-default one
           for the -z defs check. ULD: -z defs, forcing an error for undefined
           symbols, is the default for executables and not for shared objects; -G
           allows undefined symbols; -r does not complain about unresolved
           references. Two documents agree.
nomutant   runs the system's own assembler, linker and loader; faults are planted
           by substituting a wrong tool (conv/neg/run.sh, PC_CONV_AS, PC_CONV_LD,
           PC_CONV_CC)

assertion  CONV.toolchain.012
anchor     as.html#.comm
anchor     ld.html#-warn-common
anchor     llvm-nm.html#C
anchor     gabi/ch4.symtab.html#special-section-indexes
class      disputed
says       Linking several common symbols of one name gives one symbol, in zero-
           filled storage, whose size is at least the largest of them, in any
           order of the objects; linking a common symbol with a real definition
           of the name keeps the definition.
test       t/012-ld-common.sh
note       tier CONVENTION. GAS: common symbols of one name may be merged and, if
           the sizes differ, the largest is allocated. GLD: the linker merges
           common symbols of one name into one, picking the largest size, puts
           them in the uninitialised data area, and turns a common symbol into a
           declaration when there is a definition. LLNM: multiple common
           definitions link together into one. GABI: the common section index's
           size and alignment. Sources agree.
nomutant   runs the system's own assembler, linker and loader; faults are planted
           by substituting a wrong tool (conv/neg/run.sh, PC_CONV_AS, PC_CONV_LD,
           PC_CONV_CC)

assertion  CONV.toolchain.013
anchor     ld.html#-shared
anchor     uld-ld.1#-G
anchor     gabi/ch4.eheader.html#e_type
class      disputed
says       ld -shared of position-independent objects gives an object of the
           shared-object type with a dynamic section that exports its global
           functions and hides its static ones, and a program can open it with
           dlopen, find a function with dlsym, call it, and close it.
test       t/013-ld-shared.sh
note       tier CONVENTION. GLD: -shared creates a shared library. ULD: -G (and
           -shared) produces a shared object. GABI: the shared-object file type.
           The opening is done with the POSIX interfaces dlopen, dlsym and
           dlclose, whose own behaviour is not repeated here. NOTINUSE when the
           compiler refuses -fPIC or -shared or dlopen needs a library the
           compiler cannot link.
nomutant   runs the system's own assembler, linker and loader; faults are planted
           by substituting a wrong tool (conv/neg/run.sh, PC_CONV_AS, PC_CONV_LD,
           PC_CONV_CC)

assertion  CONV.toolchain.014
anchor     ld.html#-soname
anchor     uld-ld.1#-h
anchor     gabi/ch5.dynamic.html#shared-object-dependencies
class      disputed
says       ld -soname NAME records NAME as the shared object's name in its
           dynamic section, and a program linked against the object lists NAME,
           not the file name, as its dependency.
test       t/014-ld-soname.sh
note       tier CONVENTION. GLD: -soname sets the internal name that executables
           record instead of the file name used to link. ULD: -h/-soname records
           the name in the dynamic section and in objects linked with it. GABI:
           the dependency list holds the names recorded in the dynamic section
           (its example: a library with a recorded name and one without). Three
           documents agree.
nomutant   runs the system's own assembler, linker and loader; faults are planted
           by substituting a wrong tool (conv/neg/run.sh, PC_CONV_AS, PC_CONV_LD,
           PC_CONV_CC)

assertion  CONV.toolchain.015
anchor     ld.html#-rpath
anchor     uld-ld.1#-R
anchor     gabi/ch5.dynamic.html#dt_runpath
class      disputed
says       ld -rpath DIR records DIR in the dynamic section as a run path or
           library path entry; several -rpath options are joined into one colon-
           separated list in command-line order; $ORIGIN is stored as written.
test       t/015-ld-rpath.sh
note       tier CONVENTION. GLD: all -rpath arguments are concatenated and passed
           to the runtime linker; $ORIGIN is a token the path may hold. ULD:
           multiple instances are concatenated with colons; the $ORIGIN token is
           meant for relocatable objects. GABI: the run path and library path
           entries are colon-separated lists. Which of the two entry kinds is
           written is a choice of the linker (the GABI documents both; GLD notes
           the newer one wins when both exist), so either is accepted. Sources
           agree.
nomutant   runs the system's own assembler, linker and loader; faults are planted
           by substituting a wrong tool (conv/neg/run.sh, PC_CONV_AS, PC_CONV_LD,
           PC_CONV_CC)

assertion  CONV.toolchain.016
anchor     ld.html#-e
anchor     uld-ld.1#-e
anchor     gabi/ch4.eheader.html#e_entry
class      disputed
says       ld -e SYMBOL makes the entry address in the output header equal to the
           address of SYMBOL.
test       t/016-ld-entry.sh
note       tier CONVENTION. GLD: -e uses the symbol as the beginning of
           execution. ULD: sets the entry point address to the symbol. GABI: the
           header member holds the virtual address at which the system first
           transfers control. Sources agree.
nomutant   runs the system's own assembler, linker and loader; faults are planted
           by substituting a wrong tool (conv/neg/run.sh, PC_CONV_AS, PC_CONV_LD,
           PC_CONV_CC)

assertion  CONV.toolchain.017
anchor     ld.html#-r
anchor     uld-ld.1#-r
anchor     gabi/ch4.eheader.html#e_type
class      disputed
says       ld -r combines objects into one object of the relocatable type that
           keeps an unresolved reference undefined without an error, holds the
           definitions of all its inputs, and can be linked again to resolve that
           reference.
test       t/017-ld-relocatable.sh
note       tier CONVENTION. GLD: -r generates relocatable output that can serve
           as input to ld (partial linking). ULD: combines objects into one
           relocatable object and does not complain about unresolved references.
           GABI: the relocatable file type. Sources agree.
nomutant   runs the system's own assembler, linker and loader; faults are planted
           by substituting a wrong tool (conv/neg/run.sh, PC_CONV_AS, PC_CONV_LD,
           PC_CONV_CC)

assertion  CONV.toolchain.018
anchor     gabi/ch5.dynamic.html#substitution-sequences
anchor     ld.html#-rpath-link
anchor     uld-ld.1#-R
class      disputed
says       A program whose run path is $ORIGIN/../lib finds its library there
           when started from any directory and after the whole tree has been
           moved, and does not find it when copied to a tree that lacks the
           library.
test       t/018-rt-origin.sh
note       tier CONVENTION. GABI: $ORIGIN in a run path is replaced by the
           absolute directory of the object that contains it. GLD: $ORIGIN (with
           $LIB) is a token usable in search paths and means the directory
           containing the program or shared object. ULD: $ORIGIN lets dynamic
           objects be relocated to different places. Three documents agree.
           NOTINUSE when -Wl,-rpath is refused.
nomutant   runs the system's own assembler, linker and loader; faults are planted
           by substituting a wrong tool (conv/neg/run.sh, PC_CONV_AS, PC_CONV_LD,
           PC_CONV_CC)

assertion  CONV.toolchain.019
anchor     gabi/ch5.dynamic.html#shared-object-dependencies
anchor     urt-ld.so.1#LD_LIBRARY_PATH
class      disputed
says       With LD_LIBRARY_PATH naming the directory that holds a program's
           shared library (alone or in a colon-separated list that has other
           directories before it), the program starts and uses the library,
           although the program records no path to it and does not start without
           the variable.
test       t/019-rt-library-path.sh
note       tier CONVENTION. GABI: the variable holds a list of directories
           searched for the program's dependencies (before the default
           directories). URT: it enhances the search path and is a colon-
           separated list searched before the default directories. Two documents
           agree. Not asserted: whether the variable is searched before or after
           a recorded run path (GABI: before; URT does not say; the linker manual
           of GLD gives the opposite order for link time only).
nomutant   runs the system's own assembler, linker and loader; faults are planted
           by substituting a wrong tool (conv/neg/run.sh, PC_CONV_AS, PC_CONV_LD,
           PC_CONV_CC)

assertion  CONV.toolchain.020
anchor     gabi/ch4.symtab.html#symbol-visibility
anchor     ld.html#-Bsymbolic
anchor     uld-ld.1#-B-symbolic
class      disputed
says       When a program and a shared library both define a global function of
           default visibility and the library calls it, the call goes to the
           program's definition; without a definition in the program the
           library's own is used.
test       t/020-rt-preempt.sh
note       tier CONVENTION. GABI: default-visibility global symbols are
           preemptable by a definition of the same name in another component. GLD
           (-Bsymbolic): normally a program linked against a shared library can
           override the library's definition. ULD (-B symbolic): references are
           not bound at link time, so an executable or other shared object can
           override the object's own definition. Three documents agree. The
           definition and the call are built in separate objects so that the
           compiler cannot bind the call itself.
nomutant   runs the system's own assembler, linker and loader; faults are planted
           by substituting a wrong tool (conv/neg/run.sh, PC_CONV_AS, PC_CONV_LD,
           PC_CONV_CC)

assertion  CONV.toolchain.021
anchor     gabi/ch5.dynamic.html#initialization-and-termination-functions
anchor     urt-ld.so.1#initialization
anchor     x86-64-ABI/dl.tex#initialization-and-termination-functions
class      disputed
says       Functions listed in a shared library's init array have run before main
           starts, and those of a library that another library depends on run
           before the dependent library's, whatever order the program lists them
           in.
test       t/021-rt-init-order.sh
note       tier CONVENTION. GABI: after relocation each shared object runs its
           initialisation functions, all shared-object initialisation happens
           before the executable gains control, and an object's dependencies are
           initialised before it. URT: initialisation functions are called after
           relocation, in the reverse order of the topologically sorted
           dependencies, and then control passes to the application. PSABI: the
           implementation executes the init-array functions and the user program
           takes no part. Two independent documents (GABI, URT) agree; PSABI
           refers back to GABI. Not asserted: order inside one array (GABI only),
           finalisation order at exit (GABI only), what dlopen runs.
nomutant   runs the system's own assembler, linker and loader; faults are planted
           by substituting a wrong tool (conv/neg/run.sh, PC_CONV_AS, PC_CONV_LD,
           PC_CONV_CC)
